<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Filter</key>
    <dict>
        <!-- v0.7.0: WE ARE BACK IN THE KEYBOARD, DELIBERATELY.

             v0.6.0 dropped com.tencent.wetype.keyboard. That correctly
             stopped the flicker, but it also made the diagnostic useless:
             re-reading the on-device Mach-O dumps showed the 7-button cap
             machinery (WBToolbarPreferences, WBFunctionToolBar,
             WBCustomToolBarView, setToolbarFuncs:, toolbarFuncs, maxCount,
             countLimit, itemCount) lives ONLY in this extension. The host
             app carries just one policy predicate, canSetToolbarFunc:enabled:.

             So the cap cannot be found -- let alone lifted -- from the host
             app. We must run here. This build is still completely read-only
             (zero method replacement, no objc_copyClassList), which lets us
             measure whether mere presence is what flickered, or whether it
             was v0.5.0's class-list walk.

             Both processes are listed because the report is only complete
             when we can compare the two. -->
        <key>Bundles</key>
        <array>
            <string>com.tencent.wetype</string>
            <string>com.tencent.wetype.keyboard</string>
        </array>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
